# Machine-specific paths and credentials for this repo. Copy to `.env` and edit.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is GITIGNORED, and must stay that way: this repo is PUBLIC, and these are
# absolute paths and credentials on your machine.
#
# Format is `VAR=value` (no `export`, no quotes needed), which is both make-includable
# (the Makefile `-include`s this file) and shell-sourceable:
#
#   set -a && . ./.env && set +a
#
# Every value below is an EXAMPLE. Nothing here is a real path on anyone's machine -- fill in
# your own.

# Where the ORIGINAL track recordings live -- the `NNN-Artist - Title.ext` files that
# `streamalign track-mix` and the origNNN spans in `streamalign hints` correlate against
# the mix. The repo's `sources` symlink points at an external media volume that is often
# not mounted; point this at a copy that is always there.
#
# This is also where the harvester reads its Mystery Track queries from: a clip named
# `Mystery Track <N>.<wav|mp3|flac|m4a>` puts mystery N into the search. No clip, no search --
# see PROCESS.md, "Adding a new Mystery Track clip".
NETRADIO_SOURCES_DIR=/path/to/your/sources

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The directories the harvester signs (REQUIRED for the harvester)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One or more absolute directories of audio, separated by `:`. The harvester reads the TOP
# LEVEL of each -- a subdirectory is never read -- and writes nothing into any of them: it
# decodes what it finds, uploads the signatures, and records each key in its ledger. An audio
# file is `<key>.<ext>` with a `<key>.json` sidecar beside it; a file with no sidecar is not
# finished and is never read. The full contract, written for whatever fills the directories,
# is docs/HARVEST_FEED.md. A listed directory that does not exist is skipped with a row in the
# issues list, so a directory created later is picked up on the next pass.
NETRADIO_HARVEST_DIRS=/path/to/audio:/path/to/more-audio

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The harvester launcher (OPTIONAL -- scripts/run_harvester.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# All three have working defaults; set one only to move it. Each is a PLAIN NUMBER where it is
# a count -- `10MB` or `30s` is refused (exit 2), because a units suffix that was quietly
# accepted would turn the guarantee it names straight off.
#
# The interpreter the launcher runs the harvester with. Default: `.venv/bin/python` in this
# repo. The align server's launcher reads the same variable.
#NETRADIO_PYTHON=/path/to/your/.venv/bin/python
#
# The size cap on `.harvest/harvest.log`, in BYTES. At a start, a log at or over the cap is
# renamed to `harvest.log.1` -- one generation back, nothing older kept. Default: 10485760
# (10 MB). The rename happens between runs, never during one.
#NETRADIO_HARVEST_LOG_MAX_BYTES=10485760
#
# How long `stop` waits, in SECONDS, for the harvester to exit cleanly after SIGTERM before it
# resorts to `kill -9`. On SIGTERM the harvester stops the decode in flight -- that file is
# signed on a later pass, not finished now -- and then writes its state and its ledger. That
# last write is what the wait buys: a `kill -9` would leave state.json still naming the file
# it was signing (`current`) for a process that is gone. Default: 30.
#NETRADIO_HARVEST_STOP_WAIT_S=30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The signature bucket (scripts/sigstore.py; OFF until both a bucket and the AWS CLI resolve)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The harvester uploads every signature, with its sidecar beside it, to one S3-compatible
# bucket. The bucket is what makes a signature durable -- the local chroma cache is bounded
# and evicts cold entries, and the ledger is reconciled against the bucket's listing. With
# the store OFF (no bucket, or no `aws` resolvable), the harvester still signs, but the row
# carries no `uploaded_etag` and nothing reaches the bucket: the feed re-feeds the key. The
# full contract, including the dark-store behaviour, is docs/HARVEST_FEED.md.
# AWS credentials are the CLI's own concern (environment, ~/.aws/credentials, or the profile
# below); the harvester reads no credentials itself.
#NETRADIO_SIG_BUCKET=              # the signature bucket's name (REQUIRED for uploads)
#NETRADIO_AWS_CLI=                 # path to the `aws` executable; unset -> resolved from PATH
#NETRADIO_SIG_AWS_PROFILE=         # an --profile to pass the CLI; unset -> the CLI's default
#NETRADIO_SIG_S3_ENDPOINT=         # an --endpoint-url for an S3-compatible provider; unset -> default

# At every start the harvester rebuilds its ledger's rows from the bucket's listing and compares
# the two. When the keys they disagree on are more than this percent of every key with a row on
# either side, it refuses to start (a `sig_alert` of kind `ledger` in .harvest/state.json) until
# it is started once with `scripts/harvest.py --run --accept-ledger-rebuild`.
#NETRADIO_LEDGER_REBUILD_MAX_DIFF_PCT=10
# The share of signed rows (0 to 1) the reconciliation may drop the etag of, or demote for a lost
# sidecar, in one start. Past it that side is left untouched and a `sig_alert` of kind `store`
# is raised: a loss that size means the store broke, not the rows.
#NETRADIO_RECONCILE_DROP_CAP=0.10

# --- Commercial music-ID APIs (scripts/identify_by_api.py) --------------------------------
# Ask ACRCloud/AudD to name a mystery clip against their ~150-160M-track catalogues (which the
# local chroma pool can't reach). NOTE: measured to be defeated by the 1998 ISDN/EQ damage the
# same way AcoustID is -- see Archive/LESSON_acoustid_stream.md. A service is used only if its
# keys are set. Real keys go in .env (gitignored), never here.
#AUDD_API_TOKEN=            # audd.io -- 300 free requests, no card
#ACRCLOUD_HOST=            # identify-<region>.acrcloud.com  (from your ACRCloud project)
#ACRCLOUD_ACCESS_KEY=
#ACRCLOUD_ACCESS_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The cache policy (scripts/cache_budget.py; OFF until NETRADIO_CACHE_ROOT is set)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Four caches run on the machine's one cache policy (streamalign, chroma, candidates,
# stream_tracks): one directory per cache, a cap and an age limit per cache, one disk
# floor shared by every cache, and every deletion through the policy. Unset, the policy is dark:
# nothing is registered, nothing is cached or evicted — and the harvester refuses to
# start, because a signature it cannot keep is decode cost paid for nothing. Set
# only to depart from the default:
#NETRADIO_CACHE_ROOT=/path/to/your/cache-root
# The disk floor, in percent full (used / (used + free)); no cache adds an entry past
# it, and an eviction run frees entries across caches until the volume is under it.
# Default 82.
#NETRADIO_DISK_MAX_PCT=82
# Days a record stays in the policy's event log (events.jsonl, under
# NETRADIO_DOWNLOAD_ROOT when that is set, else the cache root).
# Default 30.
#NETRADIO_CACHE_EVENTS_DAYS=30
# Minutes a `.tmp`/`.part` file counts as a write in progress (kept, never evicted);
# older, it is a write that died part-way and is evicted like any entry. Default 60.
#NETRADIO_CACHE_IN_PROGRESS_MIN=60
# The decoded-array cache, `streamalign` — what an align run decodes once and re-reads
# many times, refilled by decoding again in seconds. Its settings, each optional and
# defaulting as shown:
#   NETRADIO_STREAMALIGN_CACHE_GB            cap in GB, decimal allowed   (default 4)
#   NETRADIO_STREAMALIGN_CACHE_MAX_AGE_DAYS  evict entries older than this, or `none`
#                                           (default 14)
#   NETRADIO_STREAMALIGN_CACHE_DIR           its directory ($NETRADIO_CACHE_ROOT/streamalign)
#   NETRADIO_STREAMALIGN_CACHE_HEADROOM_MB   cap slice a decode of known size may not
#                                           use (default 0)
# An align session is days, not months, so the cache ages entries out after 14 days
# without a line here; set the variable only to change that. The cap, the age limit and
# the disk floor are applied on every write to the cache (each write ends with the
# policy's eviction run for it).
#NETRADIO_STREAMALIGN_CACHE_MAX_AGE_DAYS=14
#
# The rest, each holding audio this project fetched or derived itself, and each
# refilled as described:
#
# `chroma` — the harvester's working copy of the signatures it has computed: a small
# derived matrix per analysed candidate, refilled from the signature bucket by key.
# A signature is used the moment it is computed, then lives in the bucket, so the age
# limit is the setting that matters here. A signature the bucket has not verified is
# never evicted (it is the only copy), and with the bucket unset nothing ages out:
#   NETRADIO_CHROMA_CACHE_DIR            its directory ($NETRADIO_CACHE_ROOT/chroma)
#   NETRADIO_CHROMA_CACHE_MAX_AGE_DAYS   evict entries older than this (default 14)
#   NETRADIO_CHROMA_CACHE_GB             cap in GB, decimal allowed   (default 4)
#NETRADIO_CHROMA_CACHE_MAX_AGE_DAYS=14
#
# `candidates` — the harvester's excerpt board: the best few ~30-second excerpts per
# mystery, cut where the matcher flagged so a person can rule on them by ear. Refill
# `on-play`: nothing in this repo refills an evicted excerpt; the value records that an
# excerpt can come back only when something asks to play it, and an evicted one stays
# gone until then. Within the cap the worst excerpt of a mystery goes first.
#   NETRADIO_CANDIDATES_CACHE_DIR            its directory ($NETRADIO_CACHE_ROOT/candidates)
#   NETRADIO_CANDIDATES_CACHE_GB             cap in GB, decimal allowed (default 0.25:
#                                             a dozen short excerpts per mystery are small)
#   NETRADIO_CANDIDATES_CACHE_MAX_AGE_DAYS   evict entries older than this (default 30:
#                                             a lead not listened to in a month is not a lead)
#NETRADIO_CANDIDATES_CACHE_GB=0.25
#
# `stream_tracks` — the tracks cut out of the mix as they played, reassembled across
# captures by scripts/extract_tracks.py and read by scripts/calibrate.py; refilled
# by re-cutting in minutes.
#   NETRADIO_STREAM_TRACKS_CACHE_DIR            its directory ($NETRADIO_CACHE_ROOT/stream_tracks)
#   NETRADIO_STREAM_TRACKS_CACHE_GB             cap in GB, decimal allowed (default 2: the
#                                                 flac set is a little over this, so the oldest
#                                                 re-cuts rotate out and come back by re-cut)
#   NETRADIO_STREAM_TRACKS_CACHE_MAX_AGE_DAYS   evict entries older than this (default 14:
#                                                 a calibration run is a day's work, a re-cut
#                                                 is minutes)
#NETRADIO_STREAM_TRACKS_CACHE_GB=2
#NETRADIO_STREAM_TRACKS_CACHE_MAX_AGE_DAYS=14

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The canary (scripts/harvest.py self-test; scripts/make_canary.py --key)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The harvester's self-test proves the whole process still finds a record it KNOWS it holds.
# The canary is the first calibration case (the same track `scripts/selftest.py --offline` uses).
# Its file is fed like any entry and signed once; after that, whenever the feeder puts the file
# back, the harvester re-signs it as if new, compares the new signature with the stored one
# (uploading neither), and scores it against the canary's mix and every mystery -- anything
# unexpected raises the `sig_alert` in the state file (`.harvest/state.json`).
# NETRADIO_CANARY_KEY is the key of the file the feeder re-presents.
# The key is the pool's own rule (docs/HARVEST_FEED.md): `u` + the first 20 hex of the SHA-1
# of the canary's source URL. Until this is set, the canary pass never runs and `--live`
# reports "not configured".
#
# Compute the key from the canary's URL with the one-line recipe, then set it here:
#
#   python3 -c 'import hashlib,sys; print("u" + hashlib.sha1(sys.argv[1].encode()).hexdigest()[:20])' '<the canary URL>'
#
# Keep the URL in single quotes: inside double quotes the shell would run any $(...) or
# backticks in it. A URL that itself contains a single quote needs it written as '\''.
#
# No canary.json is needed: the check defaults to the first calibration case, so it runs
# end-to-end once the key is set and the canary's signature is in the bucket.
# (A canary.json written by hand overrides the default, naming a different calibration case
# as the canary.)
#
#NETRADIO_CANARY_KEY=u<sha1(canary-url)[:20]>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The align server (scripts/align_server.py, launched by scripts/run_align.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything has a working default inside this checkout: the DSP runs in `.venv`, the
# originals are NETRADIO_SOURCES_DIR (above; `sources_local/` when unset), the hints, review
# notes and summaries live under `labels/`. Set a line only to depart from the default.
#
# The capture MP3s the inspector and the review page play, one `<stem>.mp3` per capture.
# Unset, the server lists no captures and the pages are empty.
#NETRADIO_MP3_DIR=/path/to/your/captures-mp3
#
# Where the Pass-1 `*.match.hints.tsv` files are read from. Default: this checkout's labels/.
#NETRADIO_LABELS_DIR=/path/to/your/labels
#
# The address and port the server binds. Default 127.0.0.1:8766 (loopback: another machine
# reaches it only through a proxy you put in front of it, or a bind address you set here).
#NETRADIO_ALIGN_HOST=127.0.0.1
#NETRADIO_ALIGN_PORT=8766
#
# Basic Auth on every route, when both are set. Real credentials go in .env, never here.
#NETRADIO_AUTH_USER=
#NETRADIO_AUTH_PASS=
#
# The keep-warm DSP worker is on by default; 0 leaves only the one-shot subprocess.
#NETRADIO_ALIGN_WORKER=1
#
# The server's log (read from the environment, else this file). Default: logs/align.log in
# this checkout.
#NETRADIO_ALIGN_LOG=/path/to/align.log
#
# 1 makes the server a read-only mirror: every align route answers 404, and the start-time
# cache registration and eviction are skipped. For a copy started from a worktree.
#NETRADIO_READ_ONLY=1
#
# The review sync (scripts/review_sync.py): the server commits labels/review/,
# labels/summary/ and data/rulings.json to the standing branch, pushes hourly, and
# `make review-sync` opens the one squash PR. Each setting is optional, shown with its default.
#NETRADIO_REVIEW_SYNC=1                     # 0 is the kill switch: nothing is committed or pushed
#NETRADIO_REVIEW_SYNC_BRANCH=align-review-data
#NETRADIO_REVIEW_SYNC_BASE=main
#NETRADIO_REVIEW_SYNC_REMOTE=origin
#NETRADIO_REVIEW_SYNC_COMMIT_S=900          # seconds between commit passes (at least 30)
#NETRADIO_REVIEW_SYNC_PUSH_S=3600           # seconds between pushes (at least 60)
#
# 1 lets scripts/run_align.sh start, stop or restart from a linked worktree (refused otherwise:
# a worktree's launcher would act on the live server's port). Set it in the ENVIRONMENT for one
# command; it is not read from .env, so a standing opt-out cannot sit in this file.
#NETRADIO_ALLOW_OFF_MAIN=1
#
# A pull that changes scripts/ or public/ restarts a running align server once `make hooks`
# has been run. Set this to 0 in the ENVIRONMENT (it is not read from .env) to skip one pull.
